---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/advanced/sociolinguistics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Grammar and phonology lessons treated "a language" as if it were one thing. It never is. Every language
    is a cloud of varieties differing by region, class, age, gender, ethnicity and situation — and the cloud's
    structure is the subject of sociolinguistics. Its two founding discoveries organise this lesson:
    <strong>variation is systematic</strong> (it correlates with social variables in quantifiable, lawful
    ways — the department-store study below), and <strong>prestige is assigned, not inherent</strong>
    (linguistic "correctness" tracks power, not linguistic quality). Both discoveries make the field quietly
    political, and both matter far beyond linguistics departments.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="dialect legitimacy, prestige, quantified variation, change from below" widget="equationStep" config={socioCfg} />

  <h2>Dialect versus language: a distinction power draws</h2>
  <p>
    The naive criterion — <strong>mutual intelligibility</strong> — fails immediately in both directions.
    Swedish, Norwegian and Danish are largely mutually intelligible but are counted as three languages
    (separate armies, literatures, states); "Chinese" spans varieties whose spoken forms are mutually
    unintelligible but share a writing system and a state, so they are printed as "dialects." Max Weinreich's
    famous line, relayed by an audience member: <strong>a language is a dialect with an army and a navy</strong>.
    Linguists formalise the contrast with Heinz Kloss's pair: <em>Abstand</em> languages are distant by
    structure (uncontroversially separate); <em>Ausbau</em> languages are shaped apart by elaboration — the
    same speech community standardised into distinct national languages (Serbian and Croatian are the
    textbook case). The applied lesson: when a government decides a variety is a "dialect," it is making a
    policy decision about whose children must learn school in a second code — one of the most consequential
    classifications in public life.
  </p>

  <h2>Variation is structured: the Labov programme</h2>
  <p>
    William Labov made the field quantitative. In his <strong>department-store study</strong> (New York,
    1966), he visited three stores serving different classes (Saks, Macy's, S. Klein) and asked employees
    the location of a department he could see was on "the fourth floor" — collecting hundreds of natural
    tokens of post-vocalic /r/ (the feature that separates "New York" from "Noo Yawk"). The result was a
    smooth social gradient: pronounced /r/ ran about <strong>62%</strong> at Saks, <strong>51%</strong> at
    Macy's, <strong>20%</strong> at S. Klein — class order exactly as predicted — and within every store,
    careful repetition raised /r/-pronunciation. Two regularities at once: a <em>social</em> stratification
    and a <em>stylistic</em> one. This is the variable-rule insight: a feature like r-fulness is not on/off;
    it is a probability that shifts with speaker class, attention to speech, and (later work showed) age,
    gender and network. Style-shifting itself became a measuring instrument: reading passages, minimal pairs
    and casual interview elicit the same speaker's range.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dialects have grammar.</strong> The strongest case is African American Vernacular English, long
    stigmatised as "broken." Linguistic analysis shows the opposite: AAVE <em>be</em> marks habitual aspect
    ("she be working" = she works regularly — a distinction standard English makes only with adverbs);
    copula deletion follows rules that match other English dialects' contractions (you can say "she  ø busy"
    where "she's" could contract, never where it could not); aspect markers like <em>bin</em> ("she bin
    working" = she has been working a long time) and stressed <em>been</em> encode fine-grained tense-aspect.
    AAVE is rule-governed, expressive and legitimate; children's school difficulties come from a
    <em>mismatch</em> between home and school codes, not deficit — a finding US courts took seriously (the
    1979 Ann Arbor "Black English" case required the district to train teachers in the dialect's structure).
    The same logic holds worldwide: every non-standard variety studied under the microscope has turned out
    to be a complete grammar.
  </p>

  <h2>Prestige: overt, covert, and assigned</h2>
  <p>
    If prestige were linguistic quality, the "best" variety would be identifiable by clarity, expressive
    range or beauty — and no such ranking survives analysis. What actually happens: a socially dominant
    group's variety becomes the <strong>standard</strong> through print, schooling and institutions
    (standard English congealed from the London-adjacent East Midlands dialect with the Chancery and the
    printing press), and then <em>looks</em> intrinsically better because its speakers hold the good jobs.
    Peter Trudgill's Norwich study added the crucial twist of <strong>covert prestige</strong>: in
    self-reported speech, men claimed <em>less</em> standard pronunciation than they produced — local
    working-class forms carried their own cachet (toughness, authenticity, belonging). So evaluations point
    two ways at once, and which direction dominates depends on context. The consequences are measurable
    outside linguistics: Rosina Lippi-Green's documentation shows systematic accent discrimination in
    housing, courts and hiring — listeners treat accent as a proxy for competence with startling confidence
    and no accuracy. "Proper English" is, in the analyst's flat description, the dialect of the powerful —
    useful, as any lingua franca is, but not more <em>correct</em> than any other complete grammar.
  </p>

  <h2>Diglossia, code-switching, and accommodation</h2>
  <p>
    Charles Ferguson's <strong>diglossia</strong> (1959) names societies running two functionally specialised
    varieties of one language: a high variety (formal, written, learned in school) and a low variety (home,
    market) — Classical Arabic versus colloquial Egyptian or Moroccan; Standard German versus Swiss German.
    The <strong>Arabic diglossia</strong> is the deepest case: the Qurʾan's language, MSA and a spectrum of
    colloquials coexist in one speaker's day, and moving between them is a skilled register act, not
    confusion. <strong>Code-switching</strong> — alternating languages or varieties, even mid-sentence — obeys
    grammatical constraints (Poplack's work: switches occur where the two grammars dovetail) and follows
    social logic: solidarity, topic, quotation, precision. <strong>Accommodation theory</strong> (Giles)
    explains the fine grain: speakers converge toward (or diverge from) each other's speech to manage
    distance and identity — you have done this in every job interview. Sociolinguistics therefore dissolves
    the "one language, one speaker" picture: every human is plurilingual across registers, and many across
    full languages, with the switching under fine, rule-guided control.
  </p>

  <h2>Change from below: variation as history in motion</h2>
  <p>
    Labov's first study (Martha's Vineyard, 1963) caught a sound change mid-flight: the islanders'
    centralisation of /ay/ and /aw/ (toward "rice" and "house" with backed nuclei) was strongest not among
    the oldest or youngest but among fishermen aged 31–45 with strong island identity — resistance to
    summer tourists written in phonetics. The general model: <strong>change from below</strong> enters below
    conscious awareness through inner social groups, spreads along dense networks (Lesley Milroy's Belfast
    work), and only later rises to consciousness — when it may become a <strong>change from above</strong>,
    with overt prestige and comment. Penelope Eckert's Belten High study refined the mechanism: variation
    indexes <em>social meaning</em> (her "jocks" and "burnouts" used the same vowels to mean different
    things), making speakers, not structures, the agents of change. This is the living bridge to the
    historical-linguistics lesson: the Neogrammarians' exceptionless laws and the sociolinguist's socially
    patterned variation are one process seen at two timescales.
  </p>

  <h2>Regional dialects: mapping variation in space</h2>
  <p>
    The classic regional method draws <strong>isoglosses</strong> — lines on the map on one side of which
    a feature changes. Germany's best-known is the <strong>Benrath line</strong>, separating Low German
    <em>maken</em> from High German <em>machen</em> (the consonant shift, the historical-linguistics
    lesson's subject, spreading north until it stopped); the <strong>Rhenish fan</strong> shows three
    such lines fanning out at different depths, which is precisely how a change <em>in progress</em>
    looks on a map centuries later. Dialect geography found the general truth that variation bundles
    loosely, not cleanly — which is why "dialect boundary" is always a simplification — and modern
    perceptual dialectology adds the audience's map: where people <em>think</em> dialects begin often
    tracks media and stereotypes more than acoustics. The English-speaking material is rich enough for
    the same exercises: r-fulness dividing eastern New England from the rest; the "bath vowel" splitting
    northern and southern England; yod-retention in "duke" marking Scottish and Irish speech. Region,
    class and history turn out to be axes of the same variable — Labov's department store measured, in
    one city, what the isogloss maps measured across a country.
  </p>

  <h2>Language, gender, and the critique of an idea</h2>
  <p>
    Robin Lakoff's <em>Language and Woman's Place</em> (1975) opened the field by listing "women's
    language" features — hedges, tag questions ("it's nice, isn't it?"), intensifiers — and reading them
    as learned powerlessness. Half a century of research complicated every part of that picture: women's
    reported "tentativeness" turns out to be contextual politeness work that carries conversational load;
    men dominate talk in measured turns, interruptions and airtime in mixed groups; the features are
    better described as <strong>power styles</strong> available to any speaker than as a gender register;
    and communities differ radically (women lead many sound changes, per Labov — they are often the
    innovators, not the conservers). The mature position distinguishes <strong>dominance</strong>
    approaches (language reflects and enforces hierarchy) from <strong>difference</strong> approaches
    (Tannen: distinct subcultures, mutual misreading) — with the current consensus closer to "identity
    performance": speakers use linguistic resources to position themselves as gendered beings, and the
    resources are community-specific. The lesson for analysis is the field's own: take apparent
    group-differences as data about positioning and power, not as essences.
  </p>

  <h2>Networks and communities of practice</h2>
  <p>
    Class explains coarse patterns; finer texture needs <strong>social networks</strong>. Lesley Milroy's
    Belfast work showed that a speaker's position in the local network predicts their vernacular use
    better than class alone: dense, multiplex ties (you work, drink and marry within the same few
    streets) enforce local norms — high network strength, strong local dialect; marginal members of a
    network are precisely where change enters. Penelope Eckert's Belten High study reframed the unit as
    the <strong>community of practice</strong>: the "jocks" (school-oriented) and "burnouts"
    (urban-oriented) used the same vowel variables with different social meanings, and individuals
    positioned themselves in between — categories are not boxes but trajectories. The general principle
    that survives: <strong>variation indexes identity</strong>, and identity is local, negotiated and
    mobile — which is why the same raised vowel can mean gang loyalty in one city and professional
    polish in another. Quantitative sociolinguistics' real discovery was not that society shapes speech
    (everyone knew that) but that the shaping is lawful enough to predict individual choices from
    measurable social position.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Methodologically the field has moved from variable rules to <strong>mixed-effects statistics and
    sociophonetics</strong>: individual tokens are modelled with random effects for speaker and word, and
    acoustic measurement (formants, vowel charts) replaced impressionistic coding — replication is now the
    norm. Theoretically, Eckert's "third wave" replaces categories with <strong>indexicality</strong>: a
    feature accumulates layers of social meaning (raised TRAP → careful → gay-coded → urban-professional),
    and meaning is local, negotiated, mutable — a view that connects sociolinguistics to semiotics.
    Perceptual dialectology maps where listeners <em>think</em> varieties are (boundaries drawn in the mind
    rarely match isoglosses). The applied stakes concentrate in <strong>language death and policy</strong>:
    of the world's roughly 7,000 languages, a large fraction (commonly estimated at 40–50%) are endangered,
    and with each loss goes an ecology of knowledge — taxonomies of plants, oral history, unique grammatical
    devices. Hebrew's revival is the single full success (a liturgical language nationalised in one
    century); Māori kōhanga reo immersion nests and Welsh broadcasting show the slower path; and UNESCO's
    evidence is blunt — children literate in their mother tongue learn better than children started in a
    second language, which makes mother-tongue education policy one of the highest-leverage interventions
    linguistics advises. Open questions: can attitudes to stigmatised dialects be shifted at scale (the
    evidence so far says attitudes move slowly even when knowledge does), does formal standardisation
    inevitably erode variation, and how will internet-era communities — their own registers, their own
    rapid changes — reshape the field's central models?
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Labov (1963, 1966); Lippi-Green, <em>English with an Accent</em> (1997); Trudgill, Norwich studies; Ferguson (1959); Eckert, <em>Linguistic Variation as Social Practice</em> (2000); Crystal, <em>Language Death</em> (2000).</p>
</TopicPage>
